<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">5</span>
    A roadmap to the reading
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Here is what to expect in each part of the text.</p>
<p><strong>The Preface</strong> tells you what the Groundwork is and is not. Kant distinguishes pure philosophy from empirical philosophy, ethics from natural science, and explains why he is writing a preparatory book rather than a complete system. Read the Preface, but do not worry if some of its distinctions pass by you. It will make more sense after you have seen the rest of the book.</p>
<p><strong>Chapter 1</strong> (titled "Moving from common-sense knowledge to philosophical knowledge about morality" in Bennett) is Kant's first pass at his topic. He starts with ordinary examples of moral judgment (the idea that a "good will" is the only unconditionally good thing, the concept of duty) and works his way up to a first statement of the supreme moral principle. This chapter is the most accessible in the book. It contains several famous discussions, including the shopkeeper who is honest only because honesty is good for business.</p>
<p><strong>Chapter 2</strong> (titled "Moving from popular moral philosophy to a metaphysics of morals") is the heart of the book. Kant starts over more rigorously, introduces the distinction between hypothetical and categorical imperatives, and gives multiple formulations of the categorical imperative. This is where the famous formulations appear: act only on a maxim you could will to be a universal law; treat humanity always as an end, never merely as a means. Kant also applies the formulations to four specific cases, which give you a sense of how he thinks moral reasoning works in practice.</p>
<p><strong>Chapter 3</strong> (titled "Moving from the metaphysic of morals to the critique of pure practical reason") is the hard one. Kant tries to show why we are actually bound by the categorical imperative. He argues that free will and the moral law imply each other, introduces his famous distinction between how the world appears and how it is "in itself," and claims that our sense of ourselves as free rational agents commits us to the moral law. The argument is compressed and difficult. Do what you can with it on a first reading and return to the parts that seemed most opaque.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
